<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herodotus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 484–c. 425 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herodotus was a Greek historian of the fifth century BCE, often called the father of history, whose Histories describe the peoples of the Persian world and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>. He is a key source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a>, crediting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deioces">Deioces</a> with its unification. He also described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and their four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Darius I (r. 522–486 BCE) formalised the economic administration by dividing the empire into satrapies, twenty according to <b>Herodotus</b>, each assessed a fixed annual tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herodotus</b> is said to have given readings of his work in Athens, and Thucydides wrote the history of its war with Sparta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herodotus</b> tells how the Persian general Megabazus called the people of Chalcedon blind for settling on the Asian shore when the far better site of Byzantium lay opposite.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herodotus</b> credits Glaucus of Chios with inventing the welding of iron; he made a famous iron stand for a silver bowl dedicated at Delphi by Alyattes of Lydia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>During the great wave of Greek colonisation the oracle was consulted by the founders of new cities, and tradition credits it with sanctioning, among many others, Syracuse, Croton and Cyrene, traditionally settled about 631 BCE after, as <b>Herodotus</b> tells it, repeated commands from the Pythia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 560 BCE Croesus of Lydia besieged the city; according to <b>Herodotus</b>, the Ephesians placed their town under the protection of Artemis by stretching a rope from the walls to the temple, some seven stades away, and then came to terms with him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Halicarnassus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.04,27.42&amp;year=-446" aria-label="Halicarnassus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5237 830 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Herodotus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 1100h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.04,27.42&amp;year=-446">What was here in 446 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;These neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness of night prevents from accomplishing each one the task proposed to him, with the very utmost speed.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 8 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; on the Persian royal messengers &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For if one should propose to all men a choice, bidding them select the best customs from all the customs that there are, each race of men, after examining them all, would select those of his own people…&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 3 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As it is however, if a man should say that the Athenians proved to be the saviours of Hellas, he would not fail to hit the truth…&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 7 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that the Egypt to which the Hellenes come in ships is a land which has been won by the Egyptians as an addition, and that it is a gift of the river…&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 2 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the most hateful grief of all human griefs is this, to have knowledge of the truth but no power over the event.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 9 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; spoken by a Persian guest at a banquet in Thebes before Plataea, as Herodotus heard it from Thersander of Orchomenus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for it would seem that it is easier to deceive many than one, seeing that, though he did not prove able to deceive Cleomenes the Lacedemonian by himself, yet he did this to thirty thousand Athenians.&#8221;</p><footer>Histories, book 5 &#183; tr. G. C. Macaulay, 1890 &#183; on Aristagoras of Miletus, who won the Athenians' help for the Ionian revolt after Sparta's king had refused it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
